

-- germany-battery-regulation
UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyC$# EU Battery Regulation in Germany: what sellers of battery products must know

**Short answer:** Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 applies directly in Germany and phases in duties over several years for portable, industrial, electric-vehicle, automotive and light-means-of-transport batteries, including batteries built into products. Sellers must manage substance restrictions, recycled-content and design requirements, staged labelling, QR-code and battery-passport duties, supply-chain due diligence and German producer registration.

## Key facts

- Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 is an EU regulation, so it applies directly in Germany without national transposition, and its obligations phase in over several years.
- It covers portable, industrial, electric-vehicle (EV), automotive and light-means-of-transport (LMT) batteries, including batteries incorporated in appliances, toys, tools and other products.
- Substance restrictions, recycled-content targets and performance and durability parameters apply on the dates set in the regulation; read the annexes in the consolidated text rather than relying on summaries.
- Labelling, QR-code and battery-passport information duties apply in stages, which means label artwork and supply-chain data collection must be planned early.
- Economic operators above the size thresholds must operate battery due-diligence policies for raw-material supply chains; confirm whether your company falls in scope.
- Battery producers placing batteries on the German market face national registration and take-back duties alongside the EU regulation.
- The first compliance step is always definitional: map each product against the regulation's battery categories, because the category decides which obligations apply.

## Scope: which batteries are covered

The regulation covers portable, industrial, electric-vehicle, automotive and light-means-of-transport batteries, including batteries incorporated in products. Map your products against the legal definitions first. This step matters more than it looks, because sellers often misjudge it in both directions: assuming a battery-powered product is out of scope because "we sell the device, not the battery", or assuming the strictest category applies to everything.

The regulation defines each category, and the category drives the obligations. A portable battery in a consumer device faces a different duty set than an industrial battery in energy storage or an EV battery in a vehicle. Batteries incorporated in products are covered too, so a brand selling Bluetooth speakers, power tools or e-bikes is a battery economic operator for the batteries inside, even though its core product is the device. Spare and replacement batteries sold separately are covered in their own right.

There are also exclusions and special cases in the legal text, for example for batteries in certain equipment categories, so read the scope articles rather than assuming. Where a product contains more than one battery type, assess each battery against the definitions. Document the category decision for every SKU, because every downstream duty (labelling, registration, due diligence, passport) keys off it.

## Substance and design requirements

Restricted substances, recycled-content targets and performance and durability parameters phase in on the dates set in the regulation. Read the annexes in the German EUR-Lex text; do not rely on summaries for thresholds or dates. The substance provisions restrict hazardous substances in batteries, with the detail sitting in the annexes, and the design provisions set parameters for performance, durability and, for some categories, removability and replaceability of batteries in appliances.

The practical work here is supplier-facing. You need composition data from battery and cell suppliers to demonstrate substance compliance, and performance data to meet durability parameters. Build these data requirements into purchase specifications now, because supply chains take months to respond and the information has to be traceable to the battery model. Where recycled-content targets apply, you need chain-of-custody style evidence from the material stream, not a marketing claim from a supplier slide deck.

Because obligations phase in across years, maintain an applicability calendar per battery category: which article applies from which date, and what evidence you will hold by then. A requirement that is two years away still needs supplier contracts amended today if the data takes eighteen months to flow.

## Labelling, QR codes and the battery passport

Labelling, QR-code and battery-passport information duties apply in stages. Plan label artwork and data collection early, because supply-chain data takes time to gather. The labelling provisions require specific information on the battery or its packaging, such as the information that identifies the battery type, the manufacturer, and capacity and chemistry information where the regulation requires it. The exact content and format are set in the regulation and its implementing measures, so artwork should be built from the legal text, not from a competitor's label.

The QR code links the physical battery to its digital information. For the categories where the battery passport applies, the passport is a digital record carrying the battery's key compliance, composition and lifecycle information, accessible via the QR code. Building a passport means building a data pipeline: every data element in the passport has to come from somewhere (your design records, your suppliers, your test labs), and it has to be kept current across product changes. Start by inventorying which data elements you already hold and which require new supplier obligations.

Staged application is the trap to avoid. Because labelling duties arrive in waves, a label that is compliant today can become non-compliant at the next application date. Version-control label artwork against the applicability calendar, and diary the future dates so artwork revisions are planned, not panicked.

## Due diligence for raw-material supply chains

Economic operators above the size thresholds must operate battery due-diligence policies for raw-material supply chains. Confirm whether your company falls in scope. Due diligence here means a formal policy: identifying the raw materials in scope, mapping the supply chain back toward extraction and processing, assessing social and environmental risks, and taking mitigation action where risks are found. It is a management system, not a one-off report.

Even companies below the mandatory threshold should read this section, because the duty cascades commercially. Large customers subject to due diligence will push information requests down their supply chains, so a small brand selling battery products to an in-scope customer will be asked for supply-chain data regardless of its own threshold position. Preparing a basic supply-chain map and a supplier code of conduct now is cheaper than assembling them under a customer's deadline.

Document everything. Due-diligence policies, risk assessments, supplier engagements and mitigation actions should be recorded in a system that survives staff turnover and can be shown to authorities or customers. Where independent audits or certifications are used as evidence, keep the audit reports and understand exactly what they did and did not cover.

## Producer registration and take-back in Germany

Battery producers placing batteries on the German market face national registration and take-back duties alongside the EU regulation. Verify the current German register and procedure with the Federal Environment Ministry (BMUV) guidance. The EU regulation sets the framework, but Germany operates national producer registration and collection systems, and registration is a precondition for lawful market placement, not an administrative nicety.

The take-back duty means producers must ensure end-of-life batteries are collected and treated through the appropriate systems, with reporting on quantities placed on the market and collected. Distance sellers and marketplace sellers shipping batteries or battery-powered products into Germany are caught by these duties even when they have no German establishment, typically acting through an authorised representative. Marketplaces increasingly check registration status, and non-registration is one of the fastest routes to a sales block.

Keep the registration evidence, collection-system contracts and quantity reports in a Germany-specific compliance file. Where the product also contains electronics or packaging, the battery registration sits alongside WEEE registration with Stiftung EAR and packaging registration in LUCID; a product can easily carry three parallel German registrations, and each has its own portal, deadlines and reports.

## Obligations by battery category

| Battery category | Typical products | Duty areas to assess |
|---|---|---|
| Portable | Consumer devices, power tools, toys | Substance restrictions, labelling, QR code, collection and registration |
| Industrial | Energy storage, industrial equipment | Performance and durability parameters, passport duties, due diligence |
| Electric-vehicle (EV) | Traction batteries | Passport, recycled content, performance and durability, due diligence |
| Automotive | Starter, lighting and ignition batteries | Labelling, collection, substance restrictions |
| Light means of transport (LMT) | E-bikes, e-scooters | Category-specific labelling, performance parameters, passport duties |

The table is a scoping aid, not the law. Confirm each product's category and the applicable articles in the consolidated text, because the phase-in dates differ by category and by article.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Map every SKU against the regulation's battery categories and document the decision.
- [ ] Build an applicability calendar: which articles apply to your categories, from which dates, and what evidence each needs.
- [ ] Collect substance and composition data from battery and cell suppliers; write the data duties into purchase specifications.
- [ ] Plan label artwork and QR-code implementation against the staged labelling duties; version-control artwork against future application dates.
- [ ] Inventory battery-passport data elements: what you hold, what suppliers must provide, and how the data stays current.
- [ ] Assess whether battery due-diligence duties apply to your company; map the raw-material supply chain regardless, for customer requests.
- [ ] Complete German producer registration and take-back arrangements before placing batteries on the German market; verify the current procedure in BMUV guidance.
- [ ] Keep a Germany compliance file: registration evidence, collection contracts, quantity reports, alongside WEEE and packaging registrations where applicable.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, the Battery Regulation turns the battery from a component into a regulated product with its own multi-year compliance program. The binding constraint is data: composition data, supply-chain data and passport data take longer to assemble than any other part of the program, so start supplier engagement before you finalize labels or artwork. Budget for German registration and take-back as a fixed cost of market entry, and treat the applicability calendar as a living document reviewed at least twice a year, because delegated and implementing acts continue to add detail. However, the phase-in structure also means not everything is due at once; sequencing the work by application date keeps the program manageable. Where dates, thresholds or data formats are uncertain, confirm them in the consolidated legal text rather than building on summaries.

**Frequently asked questions:**

## Does the Battery Regulation apply to batteries built into my products?

Yes. The regulation covers batteries incorporated in products, so a brand selling battery-powered devices is a battery economic operator for the batteries inside, even though its core product is the device.

## Which battery category does my product fall into?

The regulation defines portable, industrial, electric-vehicle, automotive and light-means-of-transport batteries. Map each product against the legal definitions first, because the category decides which obligations apply.

## Do I need to register in Germany if I sell from outside Germany?

Yes, in practical terms. Producers placing batteries on the German market face national registration and take-back duties, and distance sellers shipping into Germany are caught, typically acting through an authorised representative. Verify the current procedure in BMUV guidance.

## What is the battery passport?

A digital record carrying the battery's key compliance, composition and lifecycle information, accessible via a QR code on the battery. It applies in stages to the categories the regulation specifies, and building it requires a data pipeline from design records, suppliers and test labs.

## Does battery due diligence apply to small companies?

The mandatory due-diligence policy duty applies above the size thresholds set in the regulation, so confirm whether your company falls in scope. Below the threshold, expect customer-driven information requests anyway, because in-scope customers cascade the duty down their supply chains.

## Where do I find the actual application dates?

In the consolidated text of Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 on EUR-Lex. Obligations phase in across years, so always verify the application date of the specific article you rely on rather than trusting a summary.

## Assumptions and limitations

- The regulation phases obligations across years; always verify the application date of the specific article you rely on in the consolidated text.
- Delegated and implementing acts continue to add detail (for example on passport data formats and due-diligence mechanics); this guide describes the framework, not every technical detail.
- German registration procedures, portals and fees change; verify the current procedure in BMUV guidance and the register operator's publications.

## Official sources

- [Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 on EUR-Lex (English)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/1542/oj/eng)
- [European Commission: batteries and waste batteries](https://environment.ec.europa.eu/topics/waste-and-recycling/batteries_en)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

Selling battery products into Germany? [Check your product's market access requirements](/tools/can-i-sell-this) and run your battery documentation through the [document checker](/tools/document-checker) before you plan your labelling and registration timeline.
