-- germany-electronics-market-entry
UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyC$# Selling electronics in Germany: CE marking, WEEE and RoHS

**Short answer:** To sell electronics in Germany you generally need CE marking under the applicable EU directives, RoHS substance compliance, and WEEE registration with Stiftung EAR before placing products on the market. Germany adds national registration and take-back duties that catch many first-time sellers. Non-EU sellers typically act through an EU-based authorised representative or importer.

## Key facts

- Most electronic products need CE marking before sale anywhere in the EU, Germany included; the marking is the result of conformity assessment, not a standalone step.
- The typical directive set for electronics includes electromagnetic compatibility, electrical safety, radio equipment (for Bluetooth or Wi-Fi), RoHS and possibly ecodesign: map every applicable act first.
- The Market Surveillance Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 sets the enforcement framework for CE-marked products across the EU.
- Directive 2011/65/EU (RoHS) restricts hazardous substances in electrical and electronic equipment; supplier declarations are the core evidence.
- Germany implements the WEEE Directive through national law administered by Stiftung EAR (Elektro-Altgeraete Register); producers must register there before placing products on the German market and report quantities.
- Non-German sellers typically act through an authorised representative for EAR registration and related duties.
- Technical documentation and the EU Declaration of Conformity must be available to market surveillance authorities; non-EU manufacturers need an EU-based economic operator able to present them.

## CE marking: the EU baseline

Most electronic products need CE marking before sale anywhere in the EU, Germany included. Map every applicable directive or regulation first: electromagnetic compatibility, electrical safety, radio equipment (for Bluetooth/Wi-Fi), RoHS and possibly ecodesign. The Market Surveillance Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 sets the enforcement framework; its German text is on EUR-Lex.

Mapping is the step sellers most often shortcut, and it is where most failures originate. A mains-powered smart device with a wireless radio, a battery and a charger can easily fall under five or six acts at once. For each act, identify the applicable conformity assessment module: most consumer electronics use internal production control (self-assessment against harmonised standards), while certain radio equipment categories and higher-risk products may require a notified body. The route is defined per directive, so check each one rather than assuming the "main" directive covers everything.

Once the acts are mapped, the practical sequence is: select the current harmonised EN standards for each act (the versions cited in the Official Journal, which carry presumption of conformity), have an accredited laboratory test production-representative samples, and compile the results into the technical documentation. Where harmonised standards do not fully cover the product, the technical file must demonstrate how the essential requirements are met by other means, which is a heavier evidentiary burden and worth avoiding where possible.

CE marking covers series production, not just the tested sample. Put production controls in place so that every unit coming off the line matches the assessed type, and re-assess whenever the design, components, firmware or applicable standards change. German market surveillance authorities enforce at borders and on online marketplaces, and they ask for the technical documentation first.

## RoHS substance restrictions

Directive 2011/65/EU restricts hazardous substances in electrical and electronic equipment. Check the restricted-substance list and exemptions in the consolidated German text on EUR-Lex, and keep supplier declarations as evidence. RoHS is a supply-chain compliance exercise: the substances are in components, solders, coatings and cables deep in the bill of materials, so compliance is proven through supplier declarations backed by test reports for high-risk parts.

The exemption system is where RoHS gets operationally complex. The directive's annexes list exemptions for specific applications where substitution is technically impracticable, and exemptions expire or are renewed on their own schedules. A product that relies on an exemption needs to track that exemption's status; when an exemption expires, the design or the supply chain must change. Build exemption tracking into the compliance calendar alongside standards monitoring.

For sellers, the practical RoHS program has three parts: restricted-substance clauses in purchase specifications, a declaration-collection process from component suppliers (updated when suppliers change formulations), and risk-based testing of finished products or high-risk components. Market surveillance authorities can test products off the shelf, and a failure leads to withdrawal and reputational damage that dwarfs the testing budget.

## WEEE: register with Stiftung EAR before you sell

Germany implements the WEEE Directive through national law administered by Stiftung EAR (Elektro-Altgeraete Register). Producers of electrical equipment must register there before placing products on the German market and report quantities placed on the market. Start at stiftung-ear.de; non-German sellers typically act through an authorised representative.

Registration is a precondition for lawful sale, not a post-launch formality. The producer definition catches the first placer on the German market, which includes distance sellers and marketplace sellers shipping electrical equipment to German end customers from abroad. Marketplaces increasingly verify registration status, and authorities can order sales stops for unregistered products. The registration process involves identifying the equipment categories, appointing an authorised representative where required, and setting up the ongoing quantity reporting.

Quantity reporting is the recurring duty that sellers underestimate. Producers report the quantities of electrical equipment placed on the German market, typically by category, on the schedule the register sets. This means the business needs SKU-level tracking of units shipped to Germany from day one; reconstructing it later from marketplace payout reports is painful. Take-back and collection duties attach to the registration, with producers financing the collection and treatment of waste equipment through the system.

## Technical documentation and the EU Declaration of Conformity

Compile the technical file (design description, risk assessment, test reports, standards applied) and draw up the EU Declaration of Conformity. Non-EU manufacturers need an EU-based economic operator able to present these documents to market surveillance authorities.

The technical file is the product's compliance memory. It should contain the product identification and variant structure, the list of applicable directives and the harmonised standards applied, design descriptions and risk assessments, test reports from accredited laboratories, supplier declarations (notably RoHS), and the EU Declaration of Conformity itself. The declaration identifies the product, lists the directives and standards, and is signed by someone with authority in the manufacturing organization. Keep the file organized by model and variant, because authorities ask for the file of the specific model under investigation, and "we'll compile it" is not an acceptable answer.

The EU-based economic operator requirement comes from Regulation (EU) 2019/1020: for products under the listed harmonisation legislation, there must be an economic operator established in the EU who holds the declaration, cooperates with authorities and handles corrective actions. For non-EU brands this is typically the EU importer, an authorised representative appointed by written mandate, or in some cases a fulfilment service provider. The mandate's scope determines what the representative may and must do, so draft it precisely and keep the signed original on file.

## Germany-specific duties beyond CE, RoHS and WEEE

Electronics sellers in Germany face a stack of parallel national registrations. The packaging around the product triggers the Packaging Act (VerpackG): LUCID registration with the Zentrale Stelle and participation in a dual system for packaging placed on the German market. If the product contains batteries, battery producer registration and take-back duties apply alongside the EU Battery Regulation. Each regime has its own register, its own reporting and its own deadlines, and a single product can easily carry three or four German registrations.

Language duties cut across all of it. Instructions, safety information and warnings must be in German, and labelling must meet the traceability requirements: manufacturer identification, and for imported products the importer's name and address, on the product, packaging or accompanying documentation. Online listings targeting German consumers must also carry the GPSR Article 19 information set where the product falls under that regulation.

## Regime map for electronics in Germany

| Regime | What it requires | Where it is administered |
|---|---|---|
| CE marking (EMC, LVD, RED, RoHS, ecodesign as applicable) | Conformity assessment, technical file, EU Declaration of Conformity, CE mark | EU directives; enforcement by German market surveillance authorities |
| Market surveillance | EU-based economic operator; cooperation with authorities; corrective actions | Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 |
| WEEE | Registration before sale; quantity reporting; take-back financing | Stiftung EAR (stiftung-ear.de) |
| Packaging (VerpackG) | LUCID registration; dual-system participation; volume reporting | Zentrale Stelle (verpackungsregister.org) |
| Batteries | Producer registration and take-back; EU Battery Regulation duties | National register; BMUV guidance; Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 |

## Ongoing duties

Monitor standards updates, handle complaints and recalls, and keep registrations current. German market surveillance authorities enforce at borders and online marketplaces. Ongoing compliance is a program, not a project: assign ownership for standards monitoring (harmonised standards are revised and citations updated), run a complaints process that feeds safety signals back to engineering, and maintain a recall procedure that can be executed quickly, including customer communication templates and logistics arrangements.

Registrations need calendar management. EAR quantity reports, LUCID volume reports and any declarations of completeness each have their own cycles; missing a reporting deadline can trigger fines or registration issues. Review the product portfolio at least annually against the applicable legislation, because both the product range and the law change. When a supplier changes a component, treat it as a compliance event: re-verify RoHS declarations and assess whether the change affects any conformity assessment.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Map every applicable directive and regulation for each product (EMC, electrical safety, radio, RoHS, ecodesign, batteries where relevant).
- [ ] Complete conformity assessment: accredited testing to current harmonised standards, technical file, EU Declaration of Conformity, CE marking.
- [ ] Collect and file RoHS supplier declarations; track exemptions and their expiry dates.
- [ ] Register with Stiftung EAR before placing products on the German market; appoint an authorised representative if non-German.
- [ ] Register packaging in LUCID and arrange dual-system participation.
- [ ] Complete battery producer registration where the product contains batteries.
- [ ] Ensure German-language instructions, warnings and traceability labelling (manufacturer and importer identification).
- [ ] Designate the EU-based economic operator under Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 and keep the mandate on file.
- [ ] Set up complaints handling, recall procedures and a standards/legislation monitoring calendar.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, selling electronics in Germany means running EU product compliance and German registration duties as one program. The critical path is registration before sale: EAR, LUCID and battery registration must be complete before the first unit ships to a German customer, and each takes calendar time, especially when an authorised representative must be appointed. Budget for the registrations, the reporting infrastructure (SKU-level Germany shipment tracking from day one) and accredited testing as fixed market-entry costs. However, the same technical file and test reports that satisfy Germany satisfy the rest of the EU for the CE-marking layer; only the national registrations are Germany-specific, so the marginal cost of the next EU market is lower. Keep verifying the current registration procedures on stiftung-ear.de and verpackungsregister.org, because portals, categories and fees change.

**Frequently asked questions:**

## Do I need WEEE registration if I only sell through a marketplace?

Yes, if you are the producer placing electrical equipment on the German market, which includes distance sellers shipping to German end customers. Marketplaces increasingly check registration status. Non-German sellers typically act through an authorised representative.

## Which directives apply to a Wi-Fi smart home device?

Typically electromagnetic compatibility, electrical safety, the Radio Equipment Directive for the wireless function, RoHS, and possibly ecodesign or battery rules depending on the product. Map each feature (powered, radio, battery, software) to candidate legislation rather than assuming one directive covers everything.

## Can my EU importer be the economic operator under Regulation (EU) 2019/1020?

Yes. The regulation requires an economic operator established in the EU for listed harmonisation legislation; that can be the manufacturer (if EU-established), the importer, an authorised representative appointed by written mandate, or in some cases a fulfilment service provider.

## Do I need German-language manuals?

Yes. Instructions, safety information and warnings must be in the language of the member state of sale, which means German for Germany, and they must be understandable by the intended user.

## What happens if I sell without EAR registration?

Registration is a precondition for lawful market placement. Authorities can order sales stops and marketplaces can delist products; it is one of the fastest routes to an enforced halt in Germany.

## Is the CE marking enough for Germany?

No. CE marking covers the EU harmonisation layer, but Germany adds national registration duties (WEEE/EAR, packaging/LUCID, batteries) that apply in parallel. A CE-marked product without the German registrations is not ready for the German market.

## Assumptions and limitations

- This guide is a process overview. Registration steps, categories, portals and fees change; verify the current procedure on stiftung-ear.de and the consolidated directives on EUR-Lex before committing.
- Product-specific requirements live in the directives and standards themselves; this guide does not reproduce thresholds or test parameters.
- Packaging and battery duties are summarised here; see the dedicated guides for the full picture.

## Official sources

- [Stiftung EAR (German WEEE register)](https://www.stiftung-ear.de/)
- [Market Surveillance Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 on EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32019R1020)
- [RoHS Directive 2011/65/EU on EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32011L0065)
- [Radio Equipment Directive 2014/53/EU on EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32014L0053)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

